# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for the stdcxx revival
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them. The =analysis-= notes under =doc/notes= carry the
longer investigations.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* Library

The library itself, =include= and =src=: its behaviour, its interface
and what it carries that it no longer uses. The entries are binned by
the clause of the standard they fall under, in clause order, and the
tree's own housekeeping comes last. Within a bin the easiest entry is
at the top.

* Tests

The suite and its driver under =tests=: what it covers, what it
misses, and how it runs.

** TODO The suite against other libraries

The suite tests the C++03 library through clauses 18 to 27. Most of
its assertions check standard behaviour, not the library's additions.
Run against libstdc++ and libc++, a disagreement is a defect in one of
the three or a question the standard leaves open.

Fix: build the driver and the tests of clauses 21 to 27 against
another library. The tests that need the library's additions or its
=_RWSTD_= macros are listed and left out, each with the reason.

Check: the census of the tests that build, a table per library, and
each disagreement read against the standard and filed where it
belongs.

** WAITING ThreadSanitizer cannot follow =longjmp= on glibc 2.44

Under the sanitizer every =longjmp= and =siglongjmp= dies with "can't
find longjmp buf" and exit 66. A ten-line program with =sigsetjmp=
and =siglongjmp= and no signal fails the same way, under GCC 16's
libtsan and Clang 23's runtime alike. The sanitizer records the stack
pointer at =setjmp= and, at =longjmp=, demangles the one saved in the
=jmp_buf= to find the record. glibc 2.44 (a5ec880f80, "Consolidate
pointer guard to use a relro variable instead of the TCB") moved the
x86-64 pointer guard from the thread control block slot at =%fs:0x30=
to the variable =__pointer_chk_guard=; the slot now reads zero, and
the runtimes still demangle with it. The same series gave aarch64 a
rotate its runtime does not undo (78f1f0e39c). Reported upstream as
LLVM issue 228263 (2026-10); GCC's libsanitizer takes the fix at its
next merge from compiler-rt. The six programs of the spot-check note
are the ones this stops.

Fix: the sanitizer runtimes'. The tests need no change.

Check: =0.alloc=, =0.new=, =0.inputiter=, =0.outputiter=,
=18.setjmp= and =18.csetjmp= exit 0 under a sanitizer runtime that
reads the new guard.

** TODO The =limits= example expects the =traps= of an x86-64 host

=examples/manual/out/limits.out= 29 holds ~traps = true~ for
=numeric_limits<int>=. =include/limits= takes the value from the
=NO_INT_TRAPS= characterization, which divides by zero: an x86-64
host raises =SIGFPE=, so =traps= is true there; an aarch64 host
returns 0, so =config.h= defines =_RWSTD_NO_INT_TRAPS= and the example
prints false. ~make -C <build>/examples run RUN=limits~ thus reports
=DIFF= in 15D and 12D under GCC on an aarch64 host, line 29 the only
difference. =etc/config/xfail.txt= records the same =DIFF= for AIX
(=STDCXX-458=); =make run= does not read that file. Seen while the
=is_modulo= line of the example was checked, 2026-11-17.

Fix: a decision: the example stops printing =traps= of =int=; or its
expected output follows the host, a file per architecture or a line
the comparison skips; or =xfail.txt= gets an aarch64 record and the
harness reads it.

Check: ~make run RUN=limits~ in the =examples= of 15D passes on an
x86-64 and on an aarch64 host.

** TODO =rw_dblcmp= returns a 64-bit distance through =int=, and =rw_equal (1.0, 2.0)= is true

=rw_dblcmp= in =tests/src/valcmp.cpp= reads its two arguments as
integers of the size of =double= and returns their difference. Where
=double= is as wide as =long= the difference has 64 bits, and the
function returns ~int (int_diff)~, its low 32 bits. Two values whose
patterns differ by a multiple of 2^32 thus compare equal:
=rw_dblcmp (1.0, 2.0)= is 0, and so is the distance from 0.0, 0.5,
-1.0 or 7.0 to infinity. =rw_equal (double, double)= is
~0 == rw_dblcmp (x, y)~, so it calls all of them equal. The comment at
the cast says the function is not for values that far apart;
=rw_equal= hands it any two values. =rw_fltcmp= subtracts two =int=
and overflows: =rw_fltcmp (FLT_MAX, -FLT_MAX)= is -16777218, the wrong
sign. =rw_ldblcmp= saturates at =_RWSTD_INT_MAX=.
=tests/self/0.valcmp.cpp= calls none of the three. By probe in 15D
under GCC on aarch64.

In =22.locale.num.get= a =get= that never stores fails 2994 assertions
for =float=, 2910 for =long double= and 348 for =double=: 92 of the
150 storing rows of =test_floating_point= cannot fail for =double=,
and the six =double= overflow rows that expect infinity pass from a
start of 1.0. =22.locale.money.get=, =22.locale.num.put=, =26.c.math=
and =26.valarray.transcend= use the same functions. Seen while a
harness change to =22.locale.num.get= was run against a =get= that
stores nothing, 2026-11-23.

Fix: =rw_dblcmp= returns the distance saturated at =_RWSTD_INT_MAX=
and its negative, as =rw_ldblcmp= does; =rw_fltcmp= takes the
difference in a wider type and saturates the same way.

Check: =0.valcmp= gains rows for the three functions, among them 1.0
against 2.0, 0.0 against infinity, =FLT_MAX= against =-FLT_MAX= and
two adjacent values, and passes. The suite in 15D is at its pin, or
each row that moves is explained: a test that passed by this equality
fails now.

