** TODO The pure C headers

=include/ansi/_c*.h=, RW's own C90 declarations inside =std=, are the
model a strict compiler would want: exactly the C++03 C library,
whatever glibc hides. They are selected by =_RWSTD_USE_PURE_C_HEADERS=
and no configuration has compiled them since the platform retirement.
Measured under GCC 16 on 2026-11-16 (=ref-c-headers.md= chapter 7):
each of the 35 headers compiles alone, and without threads so do the
library's C++ headers. Six defects stand in the way of a build with
them:
- A thread-safe build includes =<pthread.h>=, which needs =clockid_t=
  from =<time.h>= and gets the pure =<ctime>=, which has none:
  =<string>= fails with four errors. With glibc's
  =<bits/types/clockid_t.h>= included first the library's C++ headers
  compile and a program links and runs.
- =_cstdlib.h= 132 and 138 define ~abs (long)~ and ~div (long, long)~
  inside the block for the linkage twins of =bsearch= and =qsort=,
  under =_RWSTD_NO_EXTERN_C_OVERLOAD= (107-143), which GCC defines:
  ~sizeof (std::abs (0L))~ is 4.
- =_cmath.h= declares the =double= functions alone; the =float= and
  =long double= overloads of 26.5 p5 and p6 are in the other body of
  =cmath=: ~sizeof (std::sin (0.0f))~ is 8.
- =_csetjmp.h= 36-43 makes =jmp_buf= 200 bytes on a 64-bit Linux;
  glibc's is 312 on aarch64.
- =_cstdio.h= declares =vfprintf=, =vprintf= and =vsprintf= with
  =_RWSTD_VA_LIST=, the layout-compatible =__rw_va_list= of
  =VA_LIST.cpp=, and =_cstdarg.h= defines =va_start= and =va_arg= by
  byte arithmetic. On an ABI whose =va_list= is a structure (x86_64,
  aarch64) the compiler's =va_list= converts to neither, so a call
  with a real =va_list= does not compile and the macros compute the
  wrong thing. =eccp= resolves =<stdarg.h>= to its own header first,
  so the library sees the compiler's =va_list=.
- =_cstdlib.h:127= has the C++ =qsort= call =_RWSTD_C::qsort=, and
  =bsearch= nine lines above calls its C function unqualified. Both
  stand in =namespace std= and find there the =extern "C"=
  declaration the cast to =__rw_compar_t= selects, since =_RWSTD_C=
  is =std= under every compiler (=rw/_defs.h= 478); the two compile,
  but they spell the same call two ways. The block is compiled only
  without =_RWSTD_NO_EXTERN_C_OVERLOAD=: under =eccp=, not under
  GCC.

Under =eccp -A= (11S, EDG 7.0, measured 2026-11-16,
=ref-c-headers.md= 7.2) all 35 pure headers compile, and the pure
model meets requirements the wrapper model cannot: =<cwchar>=
compiles and declares the wide I/O functions of Amendment 1,
=std::strchr= and =std::wcschr= are the const-correct pairs, and
=::atoi= is not declared after =<cstdlib>=. The =abs= and =div=
defect does not arise there, since EDG overloads on language linkage.
It fails on the =float= overloads, =va_list= and =jmp_buf=. At the tip
of 4.2.x the pure model was the one =eccp= took (=_config-eccp.h=
undefined both macros), and nothing turned it off for Sun C++ or
Compaq C++.

Fix: a decision, and the two outcomes differ for this chapter.
- The pure headers are repaired: =va_list= and the macros from the
  compiler's =<stdarg.h>=, which makes =<cstdarg>= and the
  v-functions of =<cstdio>= wrappers; the four repairs above; and
  =eccp= takes the model again. The entries on the wide I/O
  functions and on the overloads of the string functions then close
  for =eccp= by construction.
- They retire, and the first arm of every =<name.h>= wrapper goes
  with them. Each thing glibc withholds from a strict compiler is
  then declared in a wrapper behind a characterization of its own.

Check: a build with =_RWSTD_USE_PURE_C_HEADERS= under =eccp -A= and
under gcc, or the headers gone.

** TODO The C++ overloads of the wide string functions under =eccp=

glibc declares the C++ overloads of =wcschr=, =wcspbrk=, =wcsrchr=,
=wcsstr= and =wmemchr= behind =__CORRECT_ISO_CPP_WCHAR_H_PROTO=, with
=__asm= labels strict mode rejects. =CORRECT_ISO_CPP_WCHAR_H_PROTO=
characterizes whether the compiler accepts them, and =<cwchar>= and
=<wchar.h>= define the macro only when it does. Under =eccp -A= glibc
then declares the C prototypes, =<cwchar>= re-exports them, and
=21.cwchar= fails five assertions on the const overloads, as it did
under Clang before 5569cc60.

Fix: give a compiler without the glibc overloads the const-correct
pair in =std=, the way the Clang repair did.

Check: =21.cwchar= passes under =eccp -A= and its GCC and Clang rows
do not move.

** TODO =std::strchr= is not const-correct under =eccp=

14882:2003 21.4 p4 replaces =strchr= by two declarations, one taking
and returning a pointer to const and one a pointer to non-const, and
p6 to p12 do the same for =strpbrk=, =strrchr=, =strstr= and
=memchr=. glibc's =<string.h>= declares the pairs only where
=__CORRECT_ISO_CPP_STRING_H_PROTO= is defined, which it defines for
