** TODO The argument order of =unique='s predicate is an open reading

14882:2003 23.2.2.4, p19 (page 485) has =list::unique= erase each
element of a group "for which ~*i == *(i-1)~ (for the version of
unique with no arguments) or ~pred(*i, *(i - 1))~ (for the version of
unique with a predicate argument) holds", and 25.2.8, p1 (page 558)
writes the same two conditions for the algorithm: the later element
first. The published C++11 keeps the list's wording in 23.3.5.5, p19
(page 777) and turns the algorithm's in 25.3.9, p1 (page 883):
"~*(i - 1) == *i~ or ~pred(*(i - 1), *i) != false~", the earlier
element first, with p2: "The comparison function shall be an
equivalence relation." The library passes the earlier element first
in =list::unique=: ~*__first == *__next~ in =unique ()=
(=include/list.cc= 259) and ~__pred (*__first, *__next)~ in
=unique (BinaryPredicate)= (402). =std::unique= passes both orders
in one call: =adjacent_find= calls ~__pred (*__first, *__next)~
(=include/algorithm.cc= 167), the scan after it
~__pred (*__next, *__first)~ (410), and =__unique_copy=
~__pred (*__res, *__first)~ (471). Only a predicate whose result or
effects depend on the order of its arguments tells the readings
apart; =23.list.ops= passes a symmetric one. No document at hand
names an LWG issue for it. Seen while =23.list.ops= was written,
2026-11-21.

Fix: a row in the table of chapter 2 of
=doc/notes/ref-conformance.md=: the two texts of 2003 and of C++11,
the calls, and the reading the library takes, with =include/list.cc=
(=list::unique=) and =include/algorithm.cc= (=unique=,
=adjacent_find=, =__unique_copy=) as its locations. A row in a test,
with a predicate that is not symmetric, only once the reading is
decided.

Check: the row is there.

** TODO The order of equal keys in the multiple containers is unrecorded

14882:2003 Table 69 (23.1.2) says of =a_eq.insert(t)= only "inserts t
and returns the iterator pointing to the newly inserted element", and
of =a.insert(p,t)= "always inserts t in containers with equivalent
keys. always returns the iterator pointing to the element with key
equivalent to the key of t. iterator p is a hint pointing to where the
insert should start to search", amortized constant "if t is inserted
right after p": neither places t among the equal keys. C++11 Table 102
(23.2.4, printed pages 742 and 743) does: =a_eq.insert(t)= "If a range
containing elements equivalent to t exists in a_eq, t is inserted at
the end of that range", and =a.insert(p, t)= "t is inserted as close
as possible to the position just prior to p", amortized constant "if
t is inserted right before p". A probe in 15D and 11s under GCC on an
x86-64 host shows the library's choice:
- the value form keeps the equal keys in the order of insertion:
  five =insert (1/k)= into a =multimap= give 1/0 1/1 1/2 1/3 1/4,
  C++11's rule;
- the range constructor of =multimap= and =multiset= keeps them in the
  range's order: 2 1 2 3 1 2 3 3 1 2 tagged 0 to 9 give 1/1 1/4 1/8
  2/0 2/2 2/5 2/9 3/3 3/6 3/7;
- the hinted form puts t at the end of the equal range wherever p
  points: into {0/0 1/0 1/1 2/0}, 1/9 with p at 1/0, at 1/1, at 2/0,
  at =begin ()= and at =end ()= gives 0/0 1/0 1/1 1/9 2/0 each time.
  C++11 puts it before 1/0 for p at 1/0 and at =begin ()=, and
  between 1/0 and 1/1 for p at 1/1.

=doc/notes/ref-conformance.md= records none of it, and =23.set= and
=23.map= check no order among equal keys, since 2003 leaves it open.
Seen while writing those tests, 2026-11-21.

Fix: a row in =doc/notes/ref-conformance.md= that records the three
behaviours and which reading each follows; then rows in =23.map= and
=23.set= that pin the recorded behaviour, by the tags of the elements.

Check: the row; the new rows of =23.map= and =23.set= pass in 15D and
turn red with the descent of =__rb_tree::_C_insert= made to go left
at an equal key.

